---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Icons
description: QDS draws Lucide's outlined icons with qds-icon, sized by tokens and colored like the text around them.
---

<p>
  QDS uses <a href="https://lucide.dev/icons/" rel="noopener">Lucide</a>
  ({{ icons.lucideVersion }}, ISC license): over 1,500 icons drawn on one 24-pixel
  grid in a 2-pixel outline, which suits QDS's quiet look and stays legible at
  small sizes. <code>&lt;qds-icon&gt;</code> draws them.
</p>

<h2>Built-in icons</h2>

<p>
  These are registered whenever <code>@quieto/ui</code> loads, because QDS's own
  components draw them. Use any of them by name.
</p>

<ul class="icon-grid">
  {% for name in icons.builtIn %}
    <li>
      <qds-icon name="{{ name }}" size="lg"></qds-icon>
      <code>{{ name }}</code>
    </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>

<h2>Adding icons</h2>

<p>
  Any other Lucide icon is one import and one call away. Find it on
  <a href="https://lucide.dev/icons/" rel="noopener">lucide.dev</a>, import it from
  the <code>lucide</code> package, and register it under the name you will use:
</p>

<pre><code>import { registerIcons } from '@quieto/ui';
import { Trash2, Pencil } from 'lucide';

registerIcons({ trash: Trash2, pencil: Pencil });</code></pre>

<p>
  Each icon is imported on its own, so an app ships only the icons it registers,
  not the whole set. An icon already on the page that is waiting for its name
  draws as soon as it is registered, so registering after the page loads is fine.
  Registering a built-in name replaces that icon everywhere, including inside QDS
  components.
</p>

<h2>Sizes</h2>

<p>
  Three sizes, from the icon size tokens. <code>md</code> is the default.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">Token</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for size in ['sm', 'md', 'lg'] %}
        {% set token = tokens.all | findBy('name', 'qds-size-icon-' + size) %}
        <tr>
          <td><qds-icon name="search" size="{{ size }}"></qds-icon> <code>{{ size }}</code></td>
          <td><code>--qds-size-icon-{{ size }}</code></td>
          <td><code>{{ token.modes.light }}</code></td>
          <td>{{ token.description | replace('Icon: ', '') | capitalize }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2>Color</h2>

<p>
  An icon is drawn in <code>currentColor</code>: it takes the color of the text
  around it, so it follows the theme and every state of the component it sits in.
  To color one on its own, set <code>color</code> on it, from a semantic token:
</p>

{% example "Icons in the color of their text, and colored on their own" %}
  <qds-stack direction="inline" gap="400" align="center" wrap>
    <qds-button variant="danger"><qds-icon slot="start" name="x" size="sm"></qds-icon>Delete</qds-button>
    <qds-icon name="circle-check" label="Done" style="color: var(--qds-semantic-color-content-success)"></qds-icon>
    <qds-icon name="triangle-alert" label="Warning" style="color: var(--qds-semantic-color-content-warning)"></qds-icon>
  </qds-stack>
{% endexample %}

<h2>Accessibility</h2>

<p>
  An icon beside text is decorative: the text already says what it means, so the
  icon is hidden from assistive technology. That is the default.
</p>

<p>
  An icon that stands alone has to say what it means itself. Give it a
  <code>label</code>, which makes it an image with that name. On an icon-only
  button, name the button instead, with its <code>label</code> or a
  <a href="/components/tooltip/">tooltip</a>, which also shows the name on hover
  and focus:
</p>

{% example "A labelled icon, and an icon-only button named by a tooltip" %}
  <qds-stack direction="inline" gap="400" align="center">
    <qds-icon name="info" label="Information"></qds-icon>
    <qds-tooltip text="Search">
      <qds-button variant="ghost"><qds-icon name="search" size="sm"></qds-icon></qds-button>
    </qds-tooltip>
  </qds-stack>
{% endexample %}

<p>
  The <a href="/components/icon/">Icon component</a> page has its full API and a
  playground.
</p>
